            <ArticleTitle>Miscellaneous headache syndromes in children and adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-64</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article reviews headache syndromes that are uncommon and not discussed in previous articles in this issue. Cluster, temporal-mandibular joint dysfunction, occipital neuralgia, and indomethacin responsive headache, as well as several other syndromes, are discussed. Proper identification of these disorders often leads to specific treatment, resulting in dramatic relief of discomfort and pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
